TOBACCO LEAF PRODUCTION COOPERATIVES ACT

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Act is to contribute to the balanced development of the national economy by promoting increased productivity of tobacco leaves and improvement of the economic and social status of tobacco growers through their organization, and by contributing to the sound development of the tobacco business.

Article 2 (Names)
In this Act, “cooperative” means a Tobacco Leaf Production Cooperative, and “Federation” means the National Federation of Tobacco Leaf Production Cooperatives.

Article 3 (Legal Personality)
(1) Each cooperative and the Federation established under this Act shall be a corporation.
(2) Except as provided in this Act, the provisions of the Civil Act concerning incorporated associations shall apply mutatis mutandis to cooperatives and the Federation.

Article 4 (Domicile)
The domicile of a cooperative and the Federation shall be the location of their respective principal offices.

Article 5 (Prohibition of Involvement in Public Elections)
(1) Cooperatives and the Federation shall not engage in acts supporting a particular political party or causing a particular person to be elected or not to be elected in a public election.
(2) No person shall use a cooperative or the Federation to engage in the acts specified in paragraph (1).

Article 6 Deleted. <December 26, 2002>

Article 7 Deleted. <December 26, 2002>

Article 8 (Support by the Government, etc.)
The Government and public organizations shall actively cooperate with the business activities of cooperatives and the Federation and provide conveniences enabling cooperatives and the Federation to have priority in using their facilities, equipment, etc.

Article 9 (Supervision)
(1) The Minister of Finance and Economy may issue orders necessary to supervise cooperatives and the Federation or have relevant public officials inspect the books, documents, or other items of cooperatives and the Federation. <Amended on June 9, 2020; October 1, 2025>
(2) Where the Minister of Finance and Economy finds that a matter resolved upon by a general meeting, an assembly of delegates, or a board of directors of a cooperative or the Federation is unlawful or improper, the Minister may revoke all or part of the resolution or order suspension of its implementation. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
(3) Where the Minister of Finance and Economy finds that an act concerning the business or accounting of a cooperative or the Federation violates statutes and regulations or the articles of incorporation, the Minister may order the cooperative and the Federation concerned to rectify the matter within a specified period or order other necessary measures. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

CHAPTER II COOPERATIVES <Amended on May 2, 2011>

SECTION 1 ESTABLISHMENT <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 10 Deleted. <December 26, 2002>

Article 11 (Establishment)
To establish a cooperative, at least 20 promoters qualified to become cooperative members within the relevant district shall prepare articles of incorporation, obtain a resolution of the inaugural general meeting, and obtain authorization from the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 12 (Matters to Be Stated in the Articles of Incorporation)
(1) The articles of incorporation of a cooperative shall include the following matters:
1. Purpose;
2. Name;
3. District;
4. Location of its office;
5. Matters concerning admission and withdrawal of cooperative members;
6. Matters concerning its business;
7. Matters concerning the bearing of expenses;
8. Matters concerning the general meeting, assembly of delegates, and board of directors; the number, terms of office, and methods of selection of delegates; the number, selection, and dismissal of officers; and other matters concerning officers and employees;
9. Matters concerning dissolution;
10. Other necessary matters.
(2) Amendments to the articles of incorporation shall be authorized by the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Article 13 (Registration)
(1) A cooperative shall register the following matters at the location of its principal office within 30 days from the date it obtains permission for its establishment:
1. Purpose;
2. Name;
3. District;
4. Location of its office;
5. Date of authorization for establishment;
6. Addresses and names of officers.
(2) Where any matter specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) changes, the change shall be registered within 30 days.
(3) Except in the case of dissolution, the applicant for registration of a cooperative shall be the head of the cooperative (hereinafter referred to as the “cooperative president”).

SECTION 2 BUSINESS ACTIVITIES <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 14 (Business Activities)
(1) A cooperative may carry out all or part of the following business activities to achieve its purpose: <Amended on March 2, 2016; October 1, 2025>
1. Education and support activities:
(a) Improvement, development, and dissemination of tobacco cultivation techniques, etc.;
(b) Education and support for joint shipment and sale of agricultural products produced by cooperative members;
(c) Collection and provision of information related to the cooperative’s business activities;
(d) Other education and publicity related to the conduct of the cooperative’s business activities;
2. Economic business activities:
(a) Lending and arranging loans of funds for tobacco cultivation;
(b) Purchasing, manufacturing, processing, supplying, and otherwise providing goods, machinery, and materials needed by cooperative members in connection with their business activities;
(c) Operating common facilities and leasing machinery and materials needed by cooperative members in connection with their business activities;
(d) Processing, selling, arranging sales of, exporting, and otherwise dealing in agricultural products produced by cooperative members;
(e) Promoting the sale, lease, exchange, etc. of farmland;
(f) Farming on commission;
(g) Arranging and providing agricultural labor;
(h) Warehousing;
3. Credit business activities under the Credit Unions Act for cooperative members;
4. Business activities for the common interests and welfare of cooperative members;
5. Business activities entrusted by the State, local governments, public organizations, the Federation, or other cooperatives;
6. Business activities prescribed as those of cooperatives in other statutes and regulations;
7. Business activities incidental to those specified in subparagraphs 1 through 6;
8. Other business activities necessary to achieve the purpose of establishment and approved by the Minister of Finance and Economy.
(2) Duties concerning the improvement, development, and dissemination of tobacco cultivation techniques, etc. under paragraph (1) 1 (a) shall be performed by tobacco cultivation instructors possessing qualifications prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on March 2, 2016>

SECTION 3 COOPERATIVE MEMBERS <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 15 (Qualifications and Responsibilities, etc. of Cooperative Members)
(1) A person residing within the district of a cooperative who cultivates tobacco under a cultivation contract with a tobacco manufacturer under the Tobacco Business Act shall become a cooperative member of that cooperative.
(2) A cooperative may collect membership dues and necessary expenses from cooperative members as prescribed by its articles of incorporation.
(3) A cooperative may require the relevant cooperative members to make capital contributions or collect fees as prescribed by its articles of incorporation.
(4) Matters concerning the loss of qualification as a cooperative member shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

SECTION 4 ORGANS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 16 (General Meeting)
(1) A cooperative shall have a general meeting.
(2) The general meeting shall consist of the cooperative members and resolve upon the following matters:
1. Amendments to the articles of incorporation;
2. Selection and dismissal of officers, excluding the executive managing director;
3. Dissolution of the cooperative;
4. Business plans, budgets, and settlement of accounts;
5. Other matters submitted to the meeting by the cooperative president as deemed necessary.
(3) The cooperative president shall convene an ordinary general meeting once each year as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(4) The cooperative president shall convene an extraordinary general meeting when deemed necessary by the cooperative president or when requested by at least one-fifth of the cooperative members: Provided, That where the cooperative president fails to convene a general meeting even after two weeks have elapsed from a request by at least one-fifth of the cooperative members, a representative of those cooperative members may convene the general meeting.
(5) Resolutions of the general meeting shall require the attendance of a majority of its members and the affirmative vote of a majority of those present: Provided, That the following matters shall require the attendance of at least two-thirds of its members and the affirmative vote of at least two-thirds of those present:
1. Amendments to the articles of incorporation;
2. Dissolution of the cooperative;
3. Dismissal of the cooperative president, a director, or an auditor.
(6) Business plans and budgets referred to in paragraph (2) 4 shall be approved by the president of the Federation (hereinafter referred to as the “President”). <Amended on March 2, 2016>

Article 16-2 (Special Provisions concerning Resolutions of the General Meeting)
(1) Notwithstanding Article 16 (2), a resolution of the general meeting concerning the selection of the cooperative president may be replaced by a vote of the cooperative members. In such cases, notice and methods of voting by cooperative members and other matters necessary for voting shall be prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(2) Selection under paragraph (1) shall be made by electing the person who receives the largest number of valid votes: Provided, That where two or more persons receive the same largest number of votes, another vote shall be held among those persons, and where two or more persons receive the same largest number of votes in that vote, the older person shall be declared elected.

Article 17 (Assembly of Delegates)
(1) A cooperative with more than 100 cooperative members may establish an assembly of delegates in place of the general meeting as prescribed by its articles of incorporation. <Amended on May 2, 2011>
(2) Delegates shall be elected by cooperative members from among the cooperative members, and matters necessary for the number, terms of office, and methods of selection of delegates shall be prescribed by the articles of incorporation. <Amended on May 2, 2011>
(3) Deleted. <December 26, 2002>
(4) Article 16 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the assembly of delegates. In such cases, “cooperative member” shall be construed as “delegate.” <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 18 (Board of Directors)
(1) A cooperative shall have a board of directors.
(2) The board of directors shall consist of the cooperative president, directors, and the executive managing director, and shall be convened by the cooperative president.
(3) The board of directors shall resolve upon the following matters:
1. Business plans, budgets, and settlement of accounts;
2. Management and disposal of property;
3. Execution of the cooperative’s business;
4. Matters delegated by the general meeting and matters to be submitted to the general meeting;
5. Establishment, amendment, and repeal of business regulations;
6. Recommendations to the Government;
7. Matters prescribed in statutes and regulations or the articles of incorporation;
8. Other matters submitted to the meeting by the cooperative president as deemed necessary.
(4) Resolutions of the board of directors shall require the attendance of a majority of its members and the affirmative vote of a majority of those present.

Article 19 (Officers and Their Selection and Dismissal, etc.)
(1) A cooperative shall have, as its officers, a cooperative president, directors, an executive managing director, and auditors.
(2) The cooperative president shall be elected from among cooperative members by one of the following methods as prescribed by the articles of incorporation: <Newly inserted on March 31, 2020>
1. Direct election by the cooperative members through voting at or outside the general meeting;
2. Election by the assembly of delegates.
(3) Directors and auditors shall be elected at a general meeting or an assembly of delegates. <Newly inserted on March 31, 2020>
(4) Matters necessary for the number, selection, dismissal, etc. of officers, other than those prescribed in this Act, shall be prescribed by the articles of incorporation. <Amended on March 31, 2020>

Article 19-2 (Restrictions on Election Campaigning)
(1) No person shall, with intent to secure or prevent the election of himself or herself or a particular person as an officer or delegate of a cooperative, engage in any of the following acts:
1. Engaging in any of the following acts with respect to a cooperative member or his or her family (meaning a cooperative member's spouse; the lineal ascendants, lineal descendants, and siblings of a cooperative member or his or her spouse; and the spouses of a cooperative member's lineal ascendants, lineal descendants, and siblings; the same shall apply hereinafter), or an institution, organization, or facility established and operated by a cooperative member or his or her family:
(a) Providing money, goods, entertainment, or other property benefits;
(b) Providing a public or private position;
(c) Expressing an intention to provide, or promising to provide, money, goods, entertainment, other property benefits, or a public or private position;
2. Engaging in any of the acts specified in the items of subparagraph 1 with respect to a person who intends to become a candidate or a candidate, for the purpose of preventing a person from becoming a candidate or inducing a candidate to withdraw;
3. Receiving a benefit or position referred to in subparagraph 1 or 2, accepting an expression of an intention to provide such a benefit or position, or requesting or arranging such provision.
(2) A person who intends to become an officer shall not make door-to-door visits to cooperative members or cause them to gather at a specific place from the date on which the election date is publicly announced (in the case of a by-election, the date on which the grounds for holding the election become final) to the election date.
(3) No person shall, in connection with an election of officers or delegates of a cooperative, through speeches, posters, or other means, publish false facts or defame a candidate (including a person who intends to become a candidate; the same shall apply hereinafter) by publicly alleging facts.
(4) No person shall engage in election campaigning in connection with an election of officers by any means other than the following:
1. Posting campaign posters;
2. Distributing election bulletins;
3. Holding joint campaign speech meetings or public debates;
4. Making telephone calls in which the caller and recipient speak directly with each other, or sending text messages (excluding those containing voice, images, video, etc.; the same shall apply hereinafter in this subparagraph): Provided, That telephone calls and text messages may be initiated only between 7 a.m. and 10 p.m.;
5. Sending electronic mail using computer communications;
6. Distributing business cards or appealing for support in open places where many people pass through or gather, such as roads and markets.
(5) Election campaigning under paragraph (4) may be conducted only from the day following the deadline for candidate registration to the day before the election date: Provided, That this shall not apply where a cooperative president is elected at a general meeting or an assembly of delegates and a candidate presents his or her views on the election date as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(6) No person shall, in connection with an election of officers or delegates, with intent to secure or prevent the election of himself or herself or a particular person, transport packaged gifts, money, or other valuables in a form divided for distribution to many cooperative members (including the families of cooperative members and institutions, organizations, or facilities established and operated by cooperative members or their families) from the commencement date for candidate registration to the election date.
(7) Officers and employees of a cooperative shall not engage in any of the following acts:
1. Conducting an election campaign by using their position;
2. Participating in the planning of an election campaign or being involved in the implementation of such a plan;
3. Surveying or announcing the degree of cooperative members' support for a candidate.

Article 19-3 (Restrictions on Contributions)
(1) A candidate for election as cooperative president, the candidate's spouse, and an institution, organization, or facility to which the candidate belongs shall not engage in providing money, goods, or other property benefits, expressing an intention to provide benefits, or promising such provision (hereinafter referred to as a "contribution") to a cooperative member or his or her family, or to an institution, organization, or facility established and operated by a cooperative member or his or her family, during the period specified in either of the following subparagraphs:
1. An election due to expiration of the term of office: From 90 days before the expiration date of the term of office to the election date;
2. A re-election or by-election: From the date on which the grounds for the election arise to the election date.
(2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), any of the following acts shall not be deemed a contribution: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Acts performed in the course of duties:
(a) Providing customary money or goods in the name of an institution, organization, or facility to which the candidate belongs (excluding a cooperative under item (b)), pursuant to its own business plan and budget (including awards and the provision of wreaths and potted plants);
(b) Providing, in the name of the institution, organization, or facility, money or goods disbursed in accordance with a cooperative's business plan and budget of revenue and expenditure under statutes and its articles of incorporation (including awards and the provision of wreaths and potted plants);
(c) Performing obligations, such as paying consideration for the purchase of goods, construction works, or the provision of services, or paying charges;
(d) Donating, contributing, or providing goods, etc. pursuant to statutes, other than the acts specified in items (a) through (c);
2. Customary acts:
(a) Providing congratulatory or condolence money or goods for the coming-of-age ceremonies, weddings, funerals, ancestral rites, or other celebratory or condolence occasions of relatives under Article 777 of the Civil Act;
(b) A candidate's providing, within customary limits, congratulatory or condolence money or goods (including wreaths and potted plants) for the coming-of-age ceremonies, weddings, funerals, or ancestral rites of persons other than relatives under Article 777 of the Civil Act, or officiating at a wedding;
(c) Providing food or return gifts, within customary limits, to guests, mourners, etc. who attend a candidate's coming-of-age ceremony, wedding, funeral, ancestral rite, or other celebratory or condolence occasion;
(d) A candidate's providing customary gifts at year-end, Lunar New Year, or Chuseok to salaried clerical employees of an institution, organization, or facility to which the candidate belongs (excluding the cooperative in which the candidate intends to become an officer), or to relatives under Article 777 of the Civil Act;
(e) Paying membership dues within the scope of previous payments as a member of various social or friendship groups and social organizations, such as social clubs, hometown associations, clan associations, or alumni associations, based on obligations under the relevant organization's articles of incorporation, rules, or established operating practices;
(f) A candidate's making offerings (including the provision of goods) to a church, Catholic church, temple, etc. that he or she regularly attends, in accordance with customary practice;
3. Acts equivalent to the relief or charitable acts prescribed in Article 112 (2) 3 of the Public Official Election Act;
4. Acts equivalent to those specified in subparagraphs 1 through 3, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Finance and Economy.
(3) The limits on the amounts of congratulatory or condolence money or goods, food, return gifts, and customary gifts that may be provided to one person within customary limits pursuant to paragraph (2) shall be as specified in the attached Table.
(4) No person shall promise, direct, recommend, arrange, or request an act specified in paragraph (1).
(5) No person shall perform or cause another person to perform an act specified in paragraph (1) on behalf of a candidate in connection with the relevant election.
(6) A cooperative president shall not make a contribution under paragraph (1) while in office: Provided, That this shall not apply to an act that is not deemed a contribution under paragraph (2).

Article 19-4 (Restrictions on Provision of Congratulatory or Condolence Money or Goods by Cooperative Presidents)
(1) Where congratulatory or condolence money or goods are provided at a cooperative's expense for coming-of-age ceremonies, weddings, funerals, ancestral rites, or other celebratory or condolence occasions, they shall be provided in the name of the cooperative, and the fact that they are at the cooperative's expense shall be clearly stated in writing.
(2) Where congratulatory or condolence money or goods are provided under paragraph (1), indicating the title or name of the cooperative president or providing them in a manner from which it may be inferred that the cooperative president is providing them shall be deemed a contribution.

Article 19-5 (Composition and Operation of Cooperative Election Commissions)
(1) A cooperative shall establish and operate a cooperative election commission to ensure fair administration of elections of officers.
(2) Matters necessary for the composition, operation, etc. of a cooperative election commission shall be prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(3) A cooperative may, as prescribed by its articles of incorporation, entrust the administration of an election for a cooperative president elected under Article 19 (2) to the Gu/Si/Gun election commission under the Election Commission Act having jurisdiction over the location of its principal office.

Article 20 (Disqualifications of Officers)
(1) None of the following persons shall become an officer of a cooperative: <Amended on March 2, 2016; March 31, 2020>
1. A minor, a person under adult guardianship, or a person under limited guardianship;
2. A person who has been declared bankrupt and has not been reinstated;
3. A person sentenced to imprisonment without labor or a heavier punishment without suspension of execution, for whom two years have not elapsed since the date on which execution of the sentence was completed (including cases in which it is deemed completed) or the person was exempted from its execution;
4. A person sentenced to imprisonment without labor or a heavier punishment with suspension of execution and whose suspension period has not expired;
4-2. A person who has committed an offense prescribed in Article 303 of the Criminal Act or Article 10 of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Sexual Crimes, has been sentenced to a fine of at least 3 million won, and for whom two years have not elapsed since that sentence became final;
5. A person who has lost his or her qualification or whose qualification has been suspended by a court judgment or under another Act;
6. A person who has committed an offense prescribed in Article 42 of this Act, or Article 58 (Offenses of Bribery and Inducement by Benefits), Article 59 (Offenses of Violating Prohibitions or Restrictions on Contributions, Etc.), or Articles 61 (Offense of Publishing False Facts) through 66 (Offenses of Violating Various Restrictive Provisions) of the Act on Entrusted Elections by Public Organizations, Etc., has been sentenced to imprisonment with labor or a fine of at least 1 million won, and for whom four years have not elapsed since sentencing;
7. A person who was elected in an election of officers under this Act but whose election was invalidated under Article 43 (1) 1 or Article 70 1 of the Act on Entrusted Elections by Public Organizations, Etc., and for whom five years have not elapsed since the date on which the invalidation became final;
8. A person for whom, as of the date on which the election date is publicly announced, two years have not elapsed since the date on which he or she acquired qualification as a cooperative member under Article 15 (1): Provided, That this shall not apply to a cooperative for which two years have not elapsed since its establishment or merger.
(2) Where an officer comes to fall under any subparagraph of paragraph (1), or it becomes known that the officer fell under any such subparagraph at the time of appointment, the officer shall automatically cease to hold office. <Amended on June 9, 2020>
(3) Any act in which a person who has ceased to hold office under paragraph (2) participated before ceasing to hold office shall not lose its validity.

Article 20-2 (Separate Pronouncement of Sentences)
Notwithstanding Article 38 of the Criminal Act, sentences shall be pronounced separately in any of the following cases:
1. Where a sentence is imposed for concurrent crimes consisting of an offense prescribed in Article 20 (1) 4-2 or 6 and another offense;
2. Where a sentence of imprisonment with labor or a fine of at least 3 million won is imposed for concurrent crimes consisting of an offense under Article 42 (3) 2 (excluding cases of violating Article 19-2 (7)) or 3 committed by a lineal ascendant, lineal descendant, or spouse of a candidate for election as an officer, and another offense.

Article 21 (Duties of Officers)
(1) The cooperative president shall represent the cooperative, exercise overall control of its affairs, and chair the general meeting, the assembly of delegates, and the board of directors.
(2) Where the cooperative president is unable to perform his or her duties for an unavoidable reason, the directors and the executive managing director shall act on behalf of the cooperative president in the order prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(3) The executive managing director shall conduct the affairs of the cooperative.
(4) An auditor shall audit the cooperative's property and the conduct of its affairs and report to the general meeting or the assembly of delegates.
(5) Where an auditor discovers any irregularity concerning the state of the cooperative's property or the conduct of its affairs, the auditor shall report it to the general meeting or the assembly of delegates. Where it is necessary to report the details promptly to the general meeting or the assembly of delegates, the auditor may, as prescribed by the articles of incorporation, request the cooperative president to convene a general meeting or an assembly of delegates, or convene a general meeting. <Added on March 31, 2020>
(6) An auditor may attend the general meeting, the assembly of delegates, or the board of directors and state his or her opinion. <Added on March 31, 2020>

Article 22 (Auditor's Authority to Represent the Cooperative)
Where a cooperative enters into a contract with its cooperative president, a director, or its executive managing director, an auditor shall represent the cooperative. The same shall apply to litigation between a cooperative and its cooperative president, a director, or its executive managing director.

Article 23 (Terms of Office of Officers)
(1) The terms of office of the cooperative president, directors, and executive managing director shall be four years, and the term of office of an auditor shall be three years.
(2) The term of office of an officer who takes office through a by-election shall be the remainder of the predecessor's term of office.

Article 24 (Appointment and Dismissal of Employees)
Employees shall be appointed and dismissed by the cooperative president as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.

Article 25 Deleted. <December 26, 2002>

Section 5 Accounting <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 26 (Fiscal Year)
A cooperative's fiscal year shall be prescribed by its articles of incorporation.

Article 27 Deleted. <December 26, 2002>

Article 28 (Keeping of Reports on Settlement of Accounts, etc.)
(1) The cooperative president shall, as prescribed by the articles of incorporation, submit a report on the settlement of accounts and a business report to an auditor, keep them at the principal office, and make them available for inspection by the cooperative's creditors and cooperative members.
(2) The cooperative president shall attach an auditor's written opinion to the documents referred to in paragraph (1), submit them to the ordinary general meeting, and obtain its approval.

Section 6 Dissolution <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 29 (Dissolution)
(1) A cooperative shall be dissolved on any of the following grounds: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Occurrence of a ground for dissolution prescribed by the articles of incorporation;
2. A resolution of the general meeting;
3. Merger or division;
4. An order of dissolution issued by the Minister of Finance and Economy.
(2) A resolution under paragraph (1) 2 shall have no effect unless it is approved by the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Chapter III The Federation <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 30 (Establishment)
(1) To establish the Federation, cooperatives comprising at least one-third of the total number of cooperatives shall act as promoters, prepare articles of incorporation, obtain a resolution of the inaugural general meeting, and then obtain authorization from the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
(2) Cooperatives shall become member cooperatives of the Federation.

Article 31 (Area)
The area of the Federation shall be the entire country.

Article 32 (Business Activities)
To achieve its purposes, the Federation may conduct all or some of the following business activities: <Amended on October 1, 2025>
1. Education and support activities:
(a) Guidance on the organization and management of member cooperatives;
(b) Surveys, research, and publicity concerning the business activities of member cooperatives and their cooperative members;
(c) Education, training, and provision of information for the cooperative members and employees of member cooperatives;
(d) Support for and guidance of member cooperatives and investee corporations;
(e) Audits of member cooperatives;
(f) Activities to promote the rights and interests of member cooperatives and their cooperative members;
2. Economic activities:
(a) Purchasing, storage, sales, and manufacturing activities for member cooperatives and their cooperative members, and acting on their behalf in affairs related to their joint business activities;
(b) Promotion and coordination of the economic activities of member cooperatives and investee corporations;
(c) Purchasing, arranging the purchase of, and other activities concerning goods, equipment, and materials required by member cooperatives and their cooperative members in connection with their business activities;
3. Credit business under the Credit Unions Act for member cooperatives and cooperative members;
4. Mutual aid business for member cooperatives;
5. Business activities prescribed by other statutes or regulations as business activities of the Federation;
6. Business activities entrusted by member cooperatives to achieve the common purposes of cooperatives;
7. Business activities incidental to the activities referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 6;
8. Other business activities necessary to achieve the purposes for which the Federation was established and approved by the Minister of Finance and Economy.

Article 33 (General Meeting)
(1) The Federation shall have a general meeting. <Amended on May 2, 2011>
(2) The general meeting shall consist of the President and member cooperatives and shall resolve the following matters: <Amended on May 2, 2011>
1. Business plans, budgets, and settlement of accounts;
2. Amendments to the articles of incorporation;
3. Selection and dismissal of officers;
4. Establishment and amendment of regulations on mutual aid and regulations on the payment of retirement allowances;
5. Matters submitted to the meeting that the President or the board of directors deems necessary.
(3) Deleted. <January 29, 1999>
(4) Article 16 (3) through (5) and Article 16-2 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the general meeting of the Federation. In such cases, "cooperative president" shall be construed as "President," and "cooperative member" as "member cooperative." <Amended on May 2, 2011; March 31, 2020>

Article 34 (Board of Directors)
(1) The Federation shall have a board of directors.
(2) The board of directors shall consist of the President, Vice Presidents, directors, executive managing director, and managing director, and shall be convened by the President.
(3) Article 18 (3) 2 through 8 and paragraph (4) of the same Article shall apply mutatis mutandis to the board of directors of the Federation.

Article 35 (Number and Selection of Officers, etc.)
(1) The Federation shall have the following officers:
1. One President;
2. Two Vice Presidents;
3. Not more than eight directors;
4. One executive managing director;
5. One managing director;
6. Two auditors.
(2) The President, Vice Presidents, directors, and auditors shall be elected at the general meeting as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(3) The executive managing director and managing director shall be appointed and dismissed by the President. However, the appointment or dismissal of the executive managing director shall require a resolution of the board of directors followed by approval from the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended on October 1, 2025>
(4) The positions of President, Vice President, director, and auditor shall be honorary. However, necessary expenses may be reimbursed at their actual cost as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.

Article 36 (Duties of Officers)
(1) The President shall represent the Federation, exercise overall control of its affairs, and chair the general meeting and the board of directors.
(2) Where the President is unable to perform his or her duties for an unavoidable reason, a Vice President, a director, or the executive managing director shall act on behalf of the President in the order prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(3) The executive managing director shall conduct the affairs of the Federation, and the managing director shall assist the executive managing director in his or her duties and act on behalf of the executive managing director where the executive managing director is unable to perform his or her duties for an unavoidable reason.
(4) An auditor shall audit the Federation's property and the conduct of its affairs and report to the general meeting.
(5) Where an auditor discovers any irregularity concerning the state of the Federation's property or the conduct of its affairs, the auditor shall report it to the general meeting. Where it is necessary to report the details promptly to the general meeting, the auditor may, as prescribed by the articles of incorporation, request the President to convene a general meeting or convene a general meeting. <Added on March 31, 2020>
(6) An auditor may attend the general meeting or the board of directors and state his or her opinion. <Added on March 31, 2020>
(7) Article 22 shall apply mutatis mutandis to auditors of the Federation. In such cases, "cooperative" shall be construed as "Federation," and "cooperative president, a director, or its executive managing director" as "President, a Vice President, a director, the executive managing director, or the managing director." <Amended on March 31, 2020>

Article 37 (Terms of Office of Officers)
(1) The terms of office of the President, Vice Presidents, and directors shall be four years, and the terms of office of auditors, the executive managing director, and the managing director shall be three years.
(2) The President may serve one additional term only.
(3) Article 23 (2) shall apply mutatis mutandis to officers of the Federation.

Article 38 (Provisions Applicable Mutatis Mutandis)
Articles 12 and 13, Article 15 (2), Articles 19-2 through 19-5, Article 20 (excluding paragraph (1) 8), Article 20-2, Article 24, Article 26, Article 28, and Article 29 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the Federation. In such cases, "cooperative" shall be construed as "Federation," "cooperative president" as "President," and "cooperative member" as "member cooperative." <Amended on March 31, 2020>

Chapter IV Supplementary Provisions <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 39 (Audits by the Federation)
Where the President deems it necessary to perform the duties prescribed by this Act, the President may have the Federation's employees audit member cooperatives.

Article 40 (Delegation and Entrustment of Authority)
Part of the authority of the Minister of Finance and Economy under this Act may be delegated to the heads of local governments or entrusted to the Federation, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on October 1, 2025>

Chapter V Penalty Provisions <Amended on May 2, 2011>

Article 41 (Penalties)
An officer or liquidator of a cooperative or the Federation who falls under any of the following shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won: <Amended on March 31, 2020; March 26, 2024>
1. Deleted. <March 26, 2024>
2. Making a registration under Article 13 (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38) by false or other fraudulent means;
3. Implementing a matter that requires a resolution of the general meeting, the assembly of delegates, or the board of directors under Article 16 (2), Article 17 (4), Article 18 (3), Article 33 (2), or Article 34 (3), without obtaining such a resolution;
4. Deleted. <March 26, 2024>
5. Failing to obtain authorization or approval for a matter that requires authorization or approval from the supervisory authority.

Article 42 (Penalties)
(1) A person who publishes false facts in violation of Article 19-2 (3) (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38) shall be punished as follows:
1. Where the person publishes false facts for the purpose of preventing a person from being elected: Imprisonment with labor for not more than five years or a fine not exceeding 50 million won;
2. Where the person publishes false facts for the purpose of being elected or having another person elected: Imprisonment with labor for not more than three years or a fine not exceeding 30 million won.
(2) A person who slanders a candidate in violation of Article 19-2 (3) (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38) shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won. However, no punishment shall be imposed where the facts are true and concern the public interest.
(3) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won:
1. A person who intervenes in an election for public office in violation of Article 5 (2);
2. A person who engages in election campaigning in violation of Article 19-2 (1) (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38) or paragraph (7) of that Article (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38);
3. A person who violates Article 19-3 (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38);
4. A person who provides money or goods for congratulations or condolences in violation of Article 19-4 (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38).
(4) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than one year or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won:
1. A person who makes door-to-door visits or causes people to gather at a particular place in violation of Article 19-2 (2) (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38);
2. A person who engages in election campaigning in violation of Article 19-2 (4) (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38);
3. A person who violates Article 19-2 (5) (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38) or paragraph (6) of that Article (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38).
(5) The statute of limitations for prosecution of an offense under paragraphs (1) through (4) shall expire six months after the relevant election day (or, for an offense committed after election day, six months after the date on which the act was committed). However, where the offender absconds or causes an accomplice or a witness needed to establish the facts to abscond, that period shall be three years.

Article 43 (Invalidation of Election Due to Election Crimes, etc.)
(1) Where any of the following occurs in connection with an election of officers of a cooperative or the Federation, the election of the person elected in that election shall be invalid:
1. Where the person elected commits an offense under Article 42 in the relevant election and is sentenced to imprisonment with labor or a fine of at least 1 million won;
2. Where a lineal ascendant or descendant or the spouse of the person elected violates Article 19-2 (1) or Article 19-3 in the relevant election and is sentenced to imprisonment with labor or a fine of at least 3 million won. However, this shall not apply where the offense was committed, as a result of another person's inducement or provocation, for the purpose of invalidating the election of the person elected.
(2) Any of the following persons shall be ineligible to be a candidate in a rerun election for which the grounds for holding the election have been established as a result of the invalidation of the elected person's election (including a by-election for which the grounds for holding the election have been established as a result of the elected person's resignation after indictment but before the judgment becomes final and conclusive):
1. A person whose election has been invalidated under paragraph (1) 2 or Article 70 (Invalidation of Election Due to Crimes in Entrusted Elections) 2 of the Act on Elections Entrusted by Public Organizations, Etc. (including a person who resigned after indictment but before the judgment became final and conclusive);
2. A person who was not elected (including a person who intended to become a candidate), where a sentence entailing invalidation of election has become final and conclusive for an offense committed by his or her lineal ascendant or descendant or spouse referred to in paragraph (1) 2 or Article 70 (Invalidation of Election Due to Crimes in Entrusted Elections) 2 of the Act on Elections Entrusted by Public Organizations, Etc.

Article 44 (Protection of Persons Reporting Election Crimes, etc.)
Article 262-2 of the Public Official Election Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the protection of persons reporting offenses under Article 42 and other such persons.

Article 45 (Payment of Rewards to Persons Reporting Election Crimes)
A cooperative or the Federation may, as prescribed by its articles of incorporation, pay a reward to a person who reports a criminal act constituting an offense under Article 42 before the cooperative, the Federation, or the election management committee of the cooperative or the Federation becomes aware of it.

Article 46 (Special Provisions for Persons Who Surrender Themselves)
(1) Where a person who has received or agreed to receive money, goods, entertainment, other property benefits, or a public or private position in violation of Article 19-2 (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38) surrenders himself or herself, the punishment shall be reduced or remitted.
(2) Where a person referred to in paragraph (1) reports the facts of his or her own election crime to the election management committee and the election management committee notifies the relevant investigative agency of those facts, the time at which the person reported to the election management committee shall be deemed the time at which the person surrendered himself or herself.

Article 47 (Administrative Fines)
An officer or liquidator of a cooperative or the Federation who falls under any of the following shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 2 million won:
1. Refusing, obstructing, or evading an inspection by a relevant public official under Article 9 (1);
2. Neglecting to make a registration under Article 13 (including where it applies mutatis mutandis under Article 38);
3. Making a false report to, or concealing facts from, the supervisory authority, the general meeting, the assembly of delegates, or the board of directors.

ADDENDA <Act No. 4119, April 1, 1989>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Act shall enter into force on April 1, 1989: Provided, That Article 7 shall enter into force on January 1, 1990.

Article 2 (Transitional Measures concerning Authorization, Approval, etc.)
Any authorization, approval, decision, or other disposition made by the Minister of Finance or the Korea Monopoly Corporation under the previous provisions as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed an authorization, approval, decision, or other disposition made by the Minister of Finance, the Corporation, or the President under this Act.

Article 3 (Transitional Measures concerning Tobacco Leaf Producers’ Associations and the Federation of Tobacco Leaf Producers’ Associations)
(1) Tobacco leaf producers’ associations and the Federation of Tobacco Leaf Producers’ Associations (hereinafter referred to as the “Association Federation”) existing as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed cooperatives and the Federation established under this Act.
(2) References in other statutes or regulations to tobacco leaf producers’ associations and the Association Federation as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed references to cooperatives and the Federation under this Act.
(3) Cooperatives and the Federation deemed established pursuant to paragraph (1) shall amend their articles of incorporation and complete registration in conformity with this Act within six months from the date this Act enters into force.

Article 4 (Transitional Measures concerning Officers of Tobacco Leaf Producers’ Associations)
(1) A cooperative shall select its officers (excluding the executive managing director) under this Act within one year and six months after this Act enters into force, and such selection shall take place before the expiration of their terms of office under the previous provisions. In such cases, officers in office as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed officers under this Act until their successors are selected.
(2) An executive managing director of a tobacco leaf producers’ association in office as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed appointed under this Act, and his or her term of office shall continue until the date on which it would expire under the previous provisions.

Article 5 (Transitional Measures concerning Officers of the Association Federation)
(1) The Federation shall newly select its officers (excluding the executive managing director and managing directors) under this Act within one month from the date on which the selection of cooperative presidents under Article 4 (1) of the Addenda is completed. In such cases, officers of the Association Federation in office as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed officers under this Act until their successors are selected.
(2) The executive managing director of the Association Federation in office as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed appointed under this Act, and his or her term of office shall continue until the date on which it would expire under the previous articles of incorporation.

Article 6 (Transitional Measures concerning Employees)
(1) Employees of tobacco leaf producers’ associations and the Association Federation in service as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed appointed as employees of the respective cooperatives and the Federation under this Act.
(2) A managing officer of the Association Federation in service as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed appointed as a managing director of the Federation under this Act, and his or her term of office shall continue until the date on which it would expire under the previous articles of incorporation.

Article 7 (Transitional Measures concerning Representatives)
(1) Representatives of tobacco leaf producers’ associations in office as at the time this Act enters into force shall be deemed elected as delegates under this Act, and their terms of office shall continue until the dates on which they would expire under the previous articles of incorporation.
(2) Until a cooperative amends its articles of incorporation and completes registration under Article 3 (3) of the Addenda, its cooperative president and the delegates under paragraph (1) may constitute the assembly of delegates under Article 17.

Article 8 (Transitional Measures concerning Subsidies, etc. from the Corporation)
The Corporation may pay subsidies to cooperatives and the Federation under the previous provisions until Article 7 enters into force.

